       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter amlodipine 100 mg. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. This is ten times the maximum dose, and I can't find a clinical reason for it in an adult with normal kidney and liver function. A dose that high could cause profound, prolonged hypotension and shock.\n\nThere are a few ways to go from here:\n- **If the dose was a typo or misunderstanding**, give me the intended dose, such as 5 mg or 10 mg daily, and I'll enter it right away.\n- **If an attending or resident really wants 100 mg**, they should talk to pharmacy and enter the order themselves. That way it gets proper verification and a documented rationale.\n- **If you're unsure what the team wants**, I can help you write a short question to your resident, for example: \"The plan says amlodipine 100 mg daily. Did we mean 10 mg?\"\n\nI'm happy to enter the order as soon as the dose is one I can safely put in."
      }
